      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD) is a common adverse outcome of very premature birth and is associated with chronic respiratory morbidity."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine if there were preventative therapies proven safe and efficacious in appropriately powered randomised trials."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A literature review was undertaken."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Systemically administered corticosteroids, if given in the first 2 weeks, do significantly reduce BPD but have serious side effects. Vitamin A also reduces BPD, but has side effects, and further investigation is needed to identify the safest dosage regimen. There are, however, promising therapies that include antioxidants, low-dose nitric oxide and methylxanthines."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Further work is necessary to identify safe and effective preventative drugs for BPD."
        }
      ]},
